{"park":"indiana_dunes","fetched":"2026-09-06T18:14:36+00:00","attribution":{"landmarks":"© OpenStreetMap contributors, ODbL","summaries":"Wikipedia, CC BY-SA 4.0","boundary":"iNaturalist place 95241"},"landmarks":[{"id":"node/358680535","name":"Mount Baldy","kind":"peak","lon":-86.92733,"lat":41.70823,"ele_m":185.0,"wikidata":"Q16982263","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Baldy_(sand_dune)","tour":7,"summary":{"extract":"Mount Baldy is a sand dune located in Indiana Dunes National Park in Indiana, USA. It is on the southern shore of Lake Michigan and is 126 feet (38 m) tall. It is a wandering dune that moves or shifts every year, and is called a \"living dune.\"","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Baldy_(sand_dune)","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Wikipedia"},"photos":[{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f1/Indiana_dunes.jpg","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Indiana_dunes.jpg","dist_m":35,"width":640,"height":915,"license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","license_url":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","artist":"Dixi"},{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Indiana_Dunes_2023.jpg/960px-Indiana_Dunes_2023.jpg","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Indiana_Dunes_2023.jpg","dist_m":175,"width":640,"height":480,"license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","license_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","artist":"Clarinetguy097"},{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Backface_of_Baldy_P9190046.jpg/960px-Backface_of_Baldy_P9190046.jpg","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Backface_of_Baldy_P9190046.jpg","dist_m":196,"width":640,"height":480,"license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","license_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","artist":"Chris Light"},{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Backface_of_Baldy_P9190025.jpg/960px-Backface_of_Baldy_P9190025.jpg","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Backface_of_Baldy_P9190025.jpg","dist_m":196,"width":640,"height":480,"license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","license_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","artist":"Chris Light"},{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Bleak_day_on_Lake_Michigan_P6220069.jpg/960px-Bleak_day_on_Lake_Michigan_P6220069.jpg","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bleak_day_on_Lake_Michigan_P6220069.jpg","dist_m":196,"width":640,"height":480,"license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","license_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","artist":"Chris Light"},{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Mt_Baldy_from_breakwall.jpg/960px-Mt_Baldy_from_breakwall.jpg","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mt_Baldy_from_breakwall.jpg","dist_m":196,"width":640,"height":436,"license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","license_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","artist":"Chris Light"}],"street":{"image_id":"824888271785525","username":"garret","captured_at":"2015-05-25","is_pano":false,"dist_m":217,"url":"https://www.mapillary.com/app/?pKey=824888271785525","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"}},{"id":"way/431452713","name":"Long Lake","kind":"lake","lon":-87.21118,"lat":41.6149,"ele_m":null,"wikidata":"Q6672798","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Long_Lake_(Indiana)","tour":0,"summary":{"extract":"Long Lake is a large interdunal wetland in the Indiana Dunes region of Northwest Indiana. It was originally approximately 8 miles in length, but has been shortened due to development and drainage. It has a surface area of 34 hectares, and a maximum depth of 1.8 meters. There are three small islands, and the total shoreline length is 4.6 kilometers.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Long_Lake_(Indiana)","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Wikipedia"},"photos":[{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/20030525_22_South_Shore_Line_near_Ogden_Dunes%2C_IN_%285834104535%29.jpg/960px-20030525_22_South_Shore_Line_near_Ogden_Dunes%2C_IN_%285834104535%29.jpg","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20030525_22_South_Shore_Line_near_Ogden_Dunes,_IN_(5834104535).jpg","dist_m":320,"width":640,"height":480,"license":"CC BY 2.0","license_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","artist":"David Wilson"},{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/19961020_08_South_Shore_Line_%40_Ogden_Dunes%2C_IN_%285603776600%29.jpg/960px-19961020_08_South_Shore_Line_%40_Ogden_Dunes%2C_IN_%285603776600%29.jpg","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:19961020_08_South_Shore_Line_@_Ogden_Dunes,_IN_(5603776600).jpg","dist_m":382,"width":640,"height":401,"license":"CC BY 2.0","license_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","artist":"David Wilson"}],"street":null},{"id":"way/352635678","name":"Armco-Ferro-Mayflower House","kind":"historic","lon":-87.00129,"lat":41.68388,"ele_m":null,"wikidata":"Q4792939","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Armco-Ferro_House","tour":5,"summary":{"extract":"The Armco-Ferro House, in the Century of Progress Architectural District in Beverly Shores, Indiana, was originally constructed for the 1933 Century of Progress Exposition in Chicago. \"The ... Exposition opened in May of 1933 directed by the theme of science and its role in industrial advancement. Within the Home and Industrial Arts Group were model houses, which featured modern materials, building methods and innovative home appliances, including the Armco-Ferro-Mayflower, Wieboldt-Rostone and Florida Tropical houses, and the House of Tomorrow. All utilized new techniques of design, construction and prefabrication in an attempt to bring the out-of-date housing industry in line with more efficient manufacturing practices such as those used by the auto industry.\" The Home and Industrial Arts Group was the most successful venue of the Exposition.\nThe Armco-Ferro House was designed by Robert Smith Jr. of Cleveland, Ohio. It is the only remaining example from the exposition that met the Fair Committee's design criteria; a house that could be mass-produced and was affordable for an American family of modest means.From research completed by the Historic American Buildings Survey (HABS): The Ferro Enamel Corporation, one of the two major sponsors for the ... house, was formed in 1930 by a merger between the Ferro Enameling Company and the Ferro Enamel and Supply Co. The idea of using porcelain enamel for residential construction was introduced by Bob Weaver, president of the newly formed company. Shortly after the merger, Charles Bacon Rowley, architect, designed a four-person house with Ferro-Enamel shingles that the company erected in Cleveland, Ohio, in July 1932.31 Despite the innovative use of ferroenamel as a cladding material, the house was built using conventional wood construction. The first porcelain-enameled frameless steel house was completed ... in South Euclid, Ohio ... Like the Armco-Ferro house, this house was designed by Robert Smith, Jr., and was built by Insulated Steel Corporation; ...\n\nIn 1932, the American Rolling Mill Company (Armco) ... built a second porcelainenameled frameless steel house ... using Robert Smith, Jr. as architect. The Ferro Enamel Corporation and the Insulated Steel Construction Company collaborated with Armco, thus setting the stage for the partnership that made the Century of Progress home possible.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Armco-Ferro_House","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Wikipedia"},"photos":[{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Armco-Ferro_House%2C_Lake_Front_Drive%2C_Beverly_Shores%2C_IN_-_54209117164.jpg/960px-Armco-Ferro_House%2C_Lake_Front_Drive%2C_Beverly_Shores%2C_IN_-_54209117164.jpg","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Armco-Ferro_House,_Lake_Front_Drive,_Beverly_Shores,_IN_-_54209117164.jpg","dist_m":14,"width":640,"height":480,"license":"CC BY-SA 2.0","license_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","artist":"w_lemay"},{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Armco-Ferro_House%2C_Lake_Front_Drive%2C_Beverly_Shores%2C_IN_-_54209118199.jpg/960px-Armco-Ferro_House%2C_Lake_Front_Drive%2C_Beverly_Shores%2C_IN_-_54209118199.jpg","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Armco-Ferro_House,_Lake_Front_Drive,_Beverly_Shores,_IN_-_54209118199.jpg","dist_m":15,"width":640,"height":853,"license":"CC BY-SA 2.0","license_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","artist":"w_lemay"},{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Armco-Ferro_House%2C_Lake_Front_Drive%2C_Beverly_Shores%2C_IN_-_54208881011.jpg/960px-Armco-Ferro_House%2C_Lake_Front_Drive%2C_Beverly_Shores%2C_IN_-_54208881011.jpg","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Armco-Ferro_House,_Lake_Front_Drive,_Beverly_Shores,_IN_-_54208881011.jpg","dist_m":17,"width":640,"height":853,"license":"CC BY-SA 2.0","license_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","artist":"w_lemay"},{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Armco-Ferro_House%2C_Lake_Front_Drive%2C_Beverly_Shores%2C_IN_-_54209117274.jpg/960px-Armco-Ferro_House%2C_Lake_Front_Drive%2C_Beverly_Shores%2C_IN_-_54209117274.jpg","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Armco-Ferro_House,_Lake_Front_Drive,_Beverly_Shores,_IN_-_54209117274.jpg","dist_m":18,"width":640,"height":480,"license":"CC BY-SA 2.0","license_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","artist":"w_lemay"},{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Florida_House%2C_Lake_Front_Drive%2C_Beverly_Shores%2C_IN_-_54208877901.jpg/960px-Florida_House%2C_Lake_Front_Drive%2C_Beverly_Shores%2C_IN_-_54208877901.jpg","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Florida_House,_Lake_Front_Drive,_Beverly_Shores,_IN_-_54208877901.jpg","dist_m":18,"width":640,"height":853,"license":"CC BY-SA 2.0","license_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","artist":"w_lemay"},{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Florida_House%2C_Lake_Front_Drive%2C_Beverly_Shores%2C_IN_-_54209116474.jpg/960px-Florida_House%2C_Lake_Front_Drive%2C_Beverly_Shores%2C_IN_-_54209116474.jpg","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Florida_House,_Lake_Front_Drive,_Beverly_Shores,_IN_-_54209116474.jpg","dist_m":18,"width":640,"height":480,"license":"CC BY-SA 2.0","license_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","artist":"w_lemay"}],"street":null},{"id":"way/438733268","name":"Bailly Cemetery","kind":"historic","lon":-87.09164,"lat":41.63298,"ele_m":201.0,"wikidata":"Q4848474","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bailly_Cemetery","tour":2,"summary":{"extract":"Bailly Cemetery is a cemetery located at Indiana Dunes National Park in Porter, Indiana, US.\nThe cemetery is three-quarters of a mile (1.2 km) north of the Joseph Bailly Homestead on the edge of a sand ridge. The first recorded burial was Robert, the son of Joseph Bailly, in about 1827. Subsequently, the site has been considered a family graveyard. Burials of those outside the Bailly family are numerous. Today, they are all unmarked, including Swedish residents of the area. Outside the cemetery platform to the north there are eight recorded tombstones, four of which are older than 1827: Isaac Schellinger, 1811; Peter Carlbon, 1814; Rhoda Schellinger, 1816; and Thomas B. Speer, 1817.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bailly_Cemetery","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Wikipedia"},"photos":[{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Trail_Bridge_0374.jpg/960px-Trail_Bridge_0374.jpg","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trail_Bridge_0374.jpg","dist_m":332,"width":640,"height":427,"license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","license_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","artist":"Chris Light"}],"street":{"image_id":"472959417149883","username":"cookry","captured_at":"2017-06-18","is_pano":false,"dist_m":97,"url":"https://www.mapillary.com/app/?pKey=472959417149883","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"}},{"id":"way/431490556","name":"Chellberg Farm","kind":"historic","lon":-87.08905,"lat":41.62685,"ele_m":null,"wikidata":"Q5089922","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chellberg_Farm","tour":3,"summary":{"extract":"Chellberg Farm is a historic farmstead which in 1972 became part of Indiana Dunes National Park. Chellberg Farm is significant as it represents the ethnic heritage of a nearly forgotten Swedish-American settlement. The farm includes a family home, water house with windmill, chicken coop/bunkhouse, and the original barn. Other nearby Swedish landmarks have been restored or preserved, including the Burstrom Chapel and the Burstrom Cemetery.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chellberg_Farm","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Wikipedia"},"photos":[{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Lachnum_virgineum_272923111.jpg/960px-Lachnum_virgineum_272923111.jpg","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lachnum_virgineum_272923111.jpg","dist_m":94,"width":640,"height":853,"license":"CC BY 4.0","license_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0","artist":"Robert M Hallock"},{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Scarecrow.jpg/960px-Scarecrow.jpg","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Scarecrow.jpg","dist_m":95,"width":640,"height":960,"license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","license_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","artist":"Daniel Schwen"},{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Ebony_Jewelwing%2C_female%2C_Calopteryx_maculata_%283715720868%29.jpg/960px-Ebony_Jewelwing%2C_female%2C_Calopteryx_maculata_%283715720868%29.jpg","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ebony_Jewelwing,_female,_Calopteryx_maculata_(3715720868).jpg","dist_m":106,"width":640,"height":480,"license":"CC BY 2.0","license_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","artist":"Greg Schechter from San F